"He has a reply, and it is the compatibilists. The book is itself a cause. Reading an argument can change a person, and a determined world in which the Ethics is read differs from one in which it is not. Better and worse describe how fully a thing realizes its own nature and need not imply that it could have done otherwise. Whether this is enough is disputed. A second objection, pressed by Leibniz and by many since, is that his one substance swallows individuals. If I am only a wave on the sea, it becomes hard to say what makes my joys and sufferings mine, or why they should matter.": "a90c1c6436c1",
"He supported himself for the rest of his life by grinding lenses for microscopes and telescopes, work at which he was expert enough to be consulted by the scientist Christiaan Huygens. He lived in rented rooms, first in a village near Leiden and later in The Hague, owned very little, and turned down a pension from an admirer because he thought it too large. A circle of friends in Amsterdam read his writings in manuscript and discussed them by letter.": "8f2277d86cde",
"His masterpiece, the Ethics, was finished by 1675. When he went to Amsterdam to see it through the press he found that a rumor was already abroad that he had written a book proving there is no God, and he put the manuscript away. He died of a lung disease, possibly made worse by years of breathing glass dust, on February 21, 1677, at the age of forty-four. His friends carried the desk containing his papers to Amsterdam and published them within the year, with only his initials on the title page. The volume was banned a few months later. For a century afterward Spinozist was a term of abuse, and then the German Romantics rediscovered him. The man once denounced as an atheist was described by the poet Novalis as intoxicated with God.": "b7acbefb6d58",
"If everything happens necessarily, then whether I come to understand my emotions is as fixed as everything else. Spinozas book urges the reader to take a path, and his system says that the reader will take it or not as causes dictate. The same difficulty touches his own praise and blame. He calls some lives better than others and some people slaves, which seems to assume that things could have gone differently.": "7f2f24914127",
"In 1670 he published, anonymously and with a false place of printing, the Theological-Political Treatise. It argued that the Bible should be studied like any other ancient book, that its authority is moral and not scientific, and that a state is safest when its citizens are free to think and say what they like. The book was denounced across Europe. One critic called it forged in hell, and its author was soon known. In 1673 he was offered the chair of philosophy at Heidelberg, on condition that he not disturb the established religion. He declined, saying that he did not know within what limits his freedom to philosophize would have to be kept.": "8422b8e170c1",
"It might seem that such a system leaves no room for ethics. Spinozas answer is the point of the book. Each thing endeavours to persist in its own being, and our emotions are the forms this striving takes when it is helped or hindered. We are in bondage when we are pushed about by causes we do not understand. We become free, in the only sense available, as we come to understand them. An emotion, which is a passion, ceases to be a passion, as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea thereof. The more we see things as necessary, the less they have power to torment us, and the highest state is a calm joy in understanding that he calls the intellectual love of God. The last sentence of the book admits how hard this is: all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.": "b499541063d2",
"No philosopher has been read in more opposite ways. To his contemporaries the identification of God with Nature was atheism under a thin disguise: a God who has no purposes, hears no prayers, and could not have made things otherwise is no God at all. To the Romantics the same identification made everything divine. Both readings can cite the text. The geometrical form adds to the difficulty, since it hides the authors voice behind proofs, and the proofs are often less convincing than the notes he attached to them. It is safest to begin with those notes, the scholia and appendices, where he drops the method and says what he means.": "cd5d98e36851",
"Of the philosophers in this series so far, Spinoza is the one whose project most resembles the Buddhas. Both begin from the fact that people suffer through their own emotions. Both trace the suffering to ignorance of causes. Both teach that the remedy is a clear seeing of how those emotions arise, and both report that such seeing is itself the change, with no further act of will required. Spinozas proposition that a passion ceases to be a passion once it is clearly understood could stand as a description of mindfulness. Both deny that there is a free-standing self that owns its states, and both end in a calm they decline to call a reward: Blessedness is not the reward of virtue, but virtue itself.": "2bc7ef020083",
"On July 27, 1656, the congregation pronounced on him the harshest ban in its history. He was expelled and cursed by day and by night, and members were forbidden to speak with him, to do him any service, or to read anything he wrote. He was twenty-three and had published nothing. The document speaks of abominable heresies and monstrous deeds and does not say what they were. He seems to have accepted the sentence calmly, and he never sought to return.": "ae0216d5080d",
"Spinoza was born in Amsterdam in 1632, in the community of Portuguese Jews whose families had been forced to live as Christians in Iberia and had come to the Dutch Republic to practice their religion openly. His first name was Baruch, blessed, which he later Latinized as Benedictus. He was a gifted pupil in the communitys school and was expected to become a rabbi or to carry on his fathers business in dried fruit. The business failed, his father died, and by his early twenties he was studying Latin and the new philosophy of Descartes with a freethinking ex-Jesuit.": "29fcdb720eb5",
"The Ethics is laid out like Euclid, with definitions, axioms, and numbered propositions each proved from what came before. Its first conclusion is that there can be only one substance, one thing that exists in its own right, and that this substance is infinite. Spinoza calls it God or Nature. Everything else, every stone, tree, and person, is a mode of it, a way in which the one substance is for a time arranged, as a wave is a way the sea is arranged. God, on this account, did not make the world and does not stand outside it. God is the world, understood as a single self-sustaining system. And since everything follows from the nature of that system, nothing in the universe is contingent.": "e530ab3ab860",
"The difference is in the foundations. Spinozas world rests on one eternal, infinite substance, and liberation is seeing oneself as part of it, under what he calls the aspect of eternity. That is close to the Vedānta teaching that the self is one with Brahman, the view the Buddhas followers argued against for a thousand years. Buddhism denies any such substance. For the Buddha things arise in dependence on one another, and there is no further thing in which they all inhere. Spinozas remedy is also purely intellectual. He offers arguments. The Buddhist path adds a training of attention and conduct, on the view that understanding which stays in the study, as Humes did, does not reach the passions (Dharma and the West, D17).": "a09cb864482e",
